What does the passive voice have to do with this?  Your statement was
not in the passive voice; the passive-voice equivalent of what you
wrote would be something very awkward akin to:

"Before medusa is even *thought* of being done,  the needed tools will
be known: pickaxe, lockpick/skeleton key, blindfold/towel, unicorn
horn and sack of some kind for most of the potions and scrolls you are
carrying."
See, e.g., http://grammar.ccc.commnet.edu/grammar/passive.htm

What you wrote was:
"Before you *think* of doing medusa you'll now know what tools you
need: pickaxe, lockpick/skeleton key, blindfold/towel, unicorn horn
and sack of some kind for most of the potions and scrolls you are
carrying."

"Before you *think* of doing medusa" is an adverbial complement. It
modifies the independent clause "you'll now know what tools you need".

> 4. Before you *think* of doing medusa you'll now know what tools you
> need...
>
> meaning *either* they would be needed for medusa or otherwise. Neither
> is specified explicitly.

Not merely otherwise ("otherwise" could mean "after"); it denotes they
would be needed for Medusa or before thinking of doing Medusa.
Certainly it implies that Medusa should not be attempted without
having these tools or having had these tools before.  To a native
English speaker, it connotes that one should not think about doing
Medusa without these tools.

> Get it into your head: I wasn't talking explicitly about the medusa
> level full stop.

As soon as you introduce the adverbial "Before you *think* of doing
medusa" you _are_ explicitly talking about the Medusa level.



